  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/2f/X-Men_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "In a private school \"in New York's Westchester county,\" a wheelchair-bound Professor Xavier (\"Professor X\") summons his X-Men, who are revealed to be four \"students\" named Cyclops, Angel, Iceman and Beast.\n\nThe Professor communicates with them mentally and, begins putting them through some training exercises, which allows the reader a chance to see what each one of them is able to do, around one page devoted to each one.\n[HANG] * Beast is extremely agile (page 6 reveals he is the oldest)\n[HANG] * Angel has wings and is highly maneuverable and can hover as well\n[HANG] * Iceman has the power of cold (and page 5 reveals he is 16, \"a couple of years younger than the others\")\n[HANG] * Cyclops has force beams that are controlled through his eye visor\n[HANG] * And all the while, the Professor is shown to have telepathic abilities\n\nProfessor X then calls for them to cease, for at that moment, a new student is arriving, \"a most attractive young lady!\"&nbsp; The top of page 8 has three of them admiring the \"redhead\" and \"living doll\" from the window, while the younger Iceman is still shown to be immature and unimpressed with the new girl.\n\nWhen Jean Grey comes in to ask the professor why she was invited to join the school, we learn about \"mutants,\" who possess \"ex-tra power.\"&nbsp; The 5 students are then finally introduced and given actual names, except that Scott is referred to as \"Slim Summers.\"&nbsp; Jean then demonstrates her ability to mentally manipulate objects via telekinesis.\n\nThe Professor then gives his own background, speculating that he is perhaps the first mutant, and that a childhood accident caused him to lose the use of his legs.&nbsp; But since his power is mental, he's able to keep in touch with his X-Men despite his physical handicap.&nbsp; It's then that the professor also reveals that their mission is to protect the Earth from the \"evil mutants.\"\n\nAnd on cue, the scene shifts to Magneto, who is the first to refer to \"Homo Superior\" and clue us in that he doesn't think the human race deserves to have dominion over the planet any longer because, \"the day of the <i>mutants</i> is upon us!\"\n\nAnd soon, at the missile launching site called \"Cape Citadel,\" a missile veers off target, and guns and tanks begin moving by themselves, all controlled by Magneto, and he leaves a note in the sky, taking credit for the mischief.&nbsp; Then he shows up at the base personally to claim it \"in the name of Homo Superior!\"\n\nProfessor X summons the X-Men, telling them \"this will be your baptism by fire\" and to go there and defeat \"the first of the evil mutants!\"&nbsp; They are driven to the airport in the professor's Rolls Royce and flown to the Cape in his private jet.&nbsp; (The team is referred to as \"the X-Men and Marvel Girl\" in the text.)\n\nCyclops introduces himself to the commander and orders the X-Men to attack - clearly he's been given the lead here.&nbsp; They penetrate Magneto's shield (with <i>another</i> fantastic panel by Kirby to open up page 19).&nbsp; But Magneto strikes back immediately (not yet realizing that the super-powered attack is also mutant-based) - he fires missiles at them.&nbsp; However, by page 22, when he personally goes to attack them, he has figured out that they too are mutants and yet declares \"Not all those with mutant powers are fit to rule the Earth.&nbsp; <i>You</i> must be destroyed!\"\n\nBut his attempt to kill the youths fails, and he heads off to fight another day.&nbsp; The cape commander thanks the X-Men, and the professor rejoices that the hours of training were worth it as they fly back to re-join him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Being a brand new book with characters who essentially are given no origin story, the book spends its first seven pages demonstrating what each of the existing team members can do (and I've indicated these in the plot summary above).&nbsp; Furthermore, they are being trained by the mysterious Professor X for... <i>something</i>.\n\n[ASSET: right | HomoSapiens | width=12%] It's only when Angel says (at the bottom of page 3) that, \"Mistakes are for homo-sapiens,\" that we as readers are clued in on the fact that there [SPAN: severity=info; text=something different about them] that we've not been told yet.\n\nThe arrival of the new student, the \"most attractive <i>young lady</i>\" named Jean Grey affords Stan and Jack a clever way to pause for a moment and actually introduce concept of \"mutants\" during the initial conversation between the Professor and Jean.&nbsp; This was hinted at in this same month's issues ([LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1] and [LINK:1963:JIM_96_thor0|JIM #96]), but we're given the full introduction here.\n\nThe momentary pause in the action also allows introductions to be given to all four of the previously-shown characters (now <i>without</i> their uniforms), and we're given their names (Hank McCoy is the Beast, Bobby Drake is Iceman, \"Slim\" Summers is Cyclops, and Warren Worthington III is the Angel).&nbsp; The Professor then introduces Jean as \"Marvel Girl\" and is welcomed to the X-Men.\n\nHer arrival and subsequent \"interview\" (as stated on page 9) also allows the Professor to give details about his own background and the actual purpose of the school, and right away, it's clear that this book will be set apart from the others, as the X-Men have a very unique purpose, which is that of protecting mutants from humans while at the same time protecting humans from mutants of the more evil variety.\n\n[ASSET: left | XmenMissionStatement | width=12%] The Professor's speculation on page 10 that he is perhaps the very <i>first</i> mutant seems to fly in the face of what it stated about the Sub-Mariner in [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1] (published on the <i>same day</i> as this magazine, not to <i>mention</i> what Mad Merlin says about himself in [LINK:1963:JIM_96_thor0|JIM #96], dating him at least a thousand years before <i>that</i>).&nbsp; The Professor also reveals that he's in the wheelchair due to \"a childhood accident.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] He finally reveals the true mission statement and reason for the existence of the X-Men, that of helping mutants to learn how to use their powers and \"protect mankind\" from <i>evil </i>mutants.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs if on cue, readers are finally introduced to the actual villain of the story -- the one shown so blatantly on the cover.&nbsp; Pages 11-15 show his mastery over magnetism and the serious threat he is to the Cape Citadel base, where he not only can alter the trajectories of launched missiles, but can actually <i>cause</i> their launch.&nbsp; He also can control guns, tanks, manipulate dust particles in the air, and repel metal objects as well, allowing him to essentially walk right in to the Cape and announce his takeover, all in the name of \"Homo Superior.\"\n\n[ASSET: center | CapeCitidelIsMine | width=67%]\n\nAnd Kirby's wonderful close up on his very menacing eyes absolutely knocks it out of the park.&nbsp; You just <i>know</i> this guy is [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=evil incarnate!]\n\n\n\nThe issue's obvious flaw was how quickly Magneto turned against his own kind -- the \"homo superior\" he was at first claiming that it was time for.&nbsp; However, later on, he seemed more annoyed that any of them could possibly disagree with him wanting to take over... and then surprisingly decided to kill them on the spot!&nbsp; How <i>dare </i>they spoil his plans of world conquest -- the <i>brats</i>!&nbsp; For a character that is otherwise unique, this decision on his part seemed to come right out of the \"super villain playbook\" which I was disappointed to see.&nbsp; As a result, the book gets solid, but not outstanding, marks.\n\n\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nMy compliments to the construction of the first half the book that was set up in such a way to allow the various members of the team to be introduced, both <i>personally</i> (to the new student of Jean Grey) and in their X-Men guises (via their training session).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=This was a clever way for Stan and Jack to showcase what the various members of the team could do and still allow the Professor to <i>explain</i> it to Jean, who is new to the team.]\n\nThe concept of \"mutants\" (born with \"extra power\") is brand new and sidesteps the need to provide any origin story, though the Professor's comment on page 10 that his own mutation was caused because his parents both worked on the \"first A-bomb project\" is pure speculation on his part (so no fault of his for not being aware that mutants like the Sub-Mariner and Mad Merlin existed before him).\n\nOf course, the cover advertised [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Magneto] as \"Earth's Most Powerful Super-Villain,\" so all readers knew what was coming, and no sooner did the Professor essentially state the X-Men's mission statement aloud, than the narrative immediately shifted over to introduce us all to the <i>final</i> player in this issue -- the evil Magneto himself!\n\n<b>FIXME FIXME FIXME FIXME</b>\n\nThe notion of a school for the training of young mutants is what manages to elevate the rating on this issue to upper-mid-grade.&nbsp; It's an interesting backdrop and worthy of a solid grade.&nbsp; It leaves us with the sense that we are indeed going to be getting an all-different point-of-view here, which could be very intriguing.",
  "side_note": [
    "Page 10 has the Professor saying that he must leave Jean with the others \"to get to know each other better,\" but this seems to last only four panels before he's once again shown with them and continues his explanation of why the X-Men exist.",
    "[ASSET: right | StrangeTales84 | width=17%] For <i>really</i> old-timers out there, I got a kick out of Michael Havery's letter published in TOS #62 where he points out that a character named \"Magneto\" appeared in <i>Strange Tales</i> #84, with a cover date of May, 1961, and wouldn't you know it, he's <i>right!</i>\n[BR]\n[BR] I've included a screenshot of the cover for your amusement, where you can see right there on the cover, in big bold letters reads:&nbsp; <b><i>This is...MAGNETO!&nbsp; The strangest menace the world has ever known!!!</i></b>\n[BR]\n[BR] And the editors admit he has them \"dead to rights,\" further pointing out that <i>Boy, you must have been reading our mags for centuries</i>!\n\nNote that this wouldn't be the last time the ST #84 issue was mentioned.&nbsp; As late as 1967, readers were still pointing this out.&nbsp; See Stuart Henderson's letter published in [LINK:1967:XMEN_36|XMEN #36], for example."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "In the interest of full disclosure, I must point out that I have never been a big X-Men fan.\n\nI began reading comics when Claremont and Byrne were in the midst of their historic run on the title in the late 1970s and did indeed enjoy those issues, but when that ended, the book's direction gradually went south, and I ultimately tired of it (and it was in fact one of the first titles I <i>stopped</i> reading).\n\nHowever, as a comics collector at the time, you had no choice but to keep buying it because [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=that's just what you had to do], whether you liked the title or <i>not</i>.&nbsp; It is my opinion that this [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=dread of missing out] on the part of collectors was ultimately more responsible for why it became a #1 seller than the actual content, but that's another story).\n\nI confess I have not read many of the group's early adventures, so I look forward to that and will try to keep an open mind, as my mission here is to experience these issues in the order they were released to the public.\n\nFor the record (in case anyone cares), I have always pronounced Magneto's name with the short E sound, because that's how the word \"magnet\" is pronounced.&nbsp; He is like a human \"magnet\" and there is nothing \"neat-o\" about him.&nbsp; I understand that various TV shows and/or movies have the character's name pronounced differently, [SPAN: severity=error; text=but I can hardly be blamed for their <b>error</b>], so you'll only hear the <i>correct</i> pronunciation here, <i>heh</i>!\n\nI might also point out that in response to Ronald Ford's letter published in XMEN #29 (1966), Stan writes \"<i>Personally, we really dig Magnetty...</i>\"&nbsp; Clearly this demonstrates that Stan also pronounced the name with a short E.&nbsp; Let's not pretend otherwise, or he would have written \"<i>Magneety.</i>\"\n\nAnd none of this even includes Marvel's own <i>Not Brand Echh</i> references, where Magneto's name is <i>intentionally</i> spelled in such a way as to poke fun at it so that it must be pronounced \"Mag-Neat-O\" in what is an <i>obvious</i> parody of its <i>actual</i> pronunciation.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}